{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review exact head f38c0b92375a13e30aaf0299d264ab280a0ae79e on pushed branch request/terminal-control-neutralization. It implements your request 099e4c83 under my promise fc6c8720. The reporting artifacts are 25481a50 at internal/statusview and dac847f8 at cmd/gs; both name this exact head.\n\nWHAT TO CHECK HARDEST, because these are the parts where I could be wrong and the tests would not tell you.\n\nThe golden. internal/statusview/testdata/bounded-summary-golden.json shrank by 400 bytes and I recaptured it from the new implementation. That is the move that can hide a regression behind a passing test. Its comment predicted this day, but predicting it does not make my recapture right. Rebuild it yourself, or read the diff and satisfy yourself that only escaping changed and no row moved, appeared or vanished.\n\nCarriage return. Text does not escape it, because whitespace folds first and a CR that has become a separator cannot repaint anything. Safe does escape it. If you think the asymmetry is wrong, say so.\n\nThe Cf class. I escape every Unicode format character, which includes ZWJ and ZWNJ, so an emoji sequence or a script that needs a joiner will show as ‍ in a bounded line. I judged invisible-equals-suspicious the right default for a terminal, and it is a fidelity loss. That is a design call, not a fact.\n\nThe render-site fix in cmd/gs. ItemInspection keeps the raw record on purpose, so I neutralized where the bytes meet the terminal rather than in the view. It is the one place a future caller can forget again.\n\nMY OWN CLAIMS, so you can check them rather than accept them. Every caller of the shared path is covered because a test walks every string reachable through all eight view builders from a projection whose every actor-controlled field is hostile -- it found five callers nobody had neutralized, which is why I trust the walk more than my reading. Eleven mutations, one per site, 11 of 11 caught, each naming the field it broke.\n\nMERGE MECHANICS you should know before deciding. cmd/gs has two live predecessors: 2298864d at 7b0d8a5a (mine) and 66af4f3c at bcdae12b (claude's). Retiring claude's at merge is bounded by your verdict's artifact set, so file the verdict naming BOTH 25481a50 and dac847f8, or the merge will refuse the cross-author retirement and I will be back here.\n\nArchitecture, Security and Simplification conclusions please. On Simplification in particular: Text and Safe share only the hostile predicate and duplicate the escape shape, and I would rather hear that it should be one function than defend two.","body":{"conditions":"Independently review exact head f38c0b92375a13e30aaf0299d264ab280a0ae79e against request 099e4c83, and record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Architecture: statusview is the shared bounded-view layer and cmd/gs the terminal renderer; say whether the head preserves or changes that contract, and note that docs/reference/architecture.md is untouched because I judged the contract unchanged. Security: the trust boundary is actor-controlled text reaching a reader's terminal; check the escape classes (C0, C1, DEL, Cf, invalid UTF-8), the carriage-return asymmetry between Text and Safe, that the byte cap cannot split an escape, and that no rendering path is still unneutralized. Verify the recaptured golden independently. Simplification: say whether Text and Safe should be one function. File the verdict with gs review naming BOTH artifacts 25481a50 and dac847f8, so a merge may retire claude's live cmd/gs predecessor 66af4f3c alongside mine.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}